WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.125
TODD PRIVES: Hey, everyone.

00:00:01.125 --> 00:00:01.990
I'm Todd Prives.

00:00:01.990 --> 00:00:04.880
I'm a product manager on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.330
And I've spent my
entire 13-year career

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:08.940
working in visual
effects software.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.950
I've always been
driven by the desire

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.580
to make tools to make
people's work better.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.920
I've never quite considered
myself to be an artist.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.190
So what drives me is helping
other people succeed.

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:22.140
And the fact that I've been
able to join Google Cloud

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.900
Platform through the
acquisition of Zync in September

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.030
is really exciting.

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:30.050
That Google is so
invested in this space,

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.560
and now to be able to be
standing here in front of you,

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.380
seeing all these people,
a lot of whom I've

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:37.009
worked with and known
for many, many years,

00:00:37.009 --> 00:00:39.050
talking about the solutions
we're working towards

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:41.870
to make your lives better, to
make your work more efficient,

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:46.250
and to really improve what you
do, is really exciting to me.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:48.680
And I'm just really happy
to be here as part of Google

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.510
right now.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:52.600
So as [? Belle ?]
touched on before,

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.205
Google Cloud Platform is an
incredible infrastructure.

00:00:55.205 --> 00:00:58.610
And it was really new for me
as well when I joined Google,

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:03.140
to understand what we're capable
of, what the scalability is,

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.285
and what you can achieve.

00:01:04.285 --> 00:01:05.660
And the way I like
to describe it

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:08.710
is, it's the same infrastructure
that powers Google today.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:10.540
So it's the same
infrastructure that

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:14.580
handles billions of search
queries on a daily basis.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:17.290
It's the same
infrastructure that's

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:20.120
protected by over 500
security professionals whose

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.330
whole goal is to prevent
nation-state-sponsored attacks

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:23.830
from Google.

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:25.920
When we air the interview
on Christmas Day,

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.890
you can imagine the concerns
about what sort of hacking

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:29.960
might go on.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.800
This is Google's bread and
butter, securing your data,

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.179
protecting your data.

00:01:34.179 --> 00:01:35.720
And it's the same
infrastructure that

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:37.880
handles countless hours
of video every day

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.950
that are uploaded, transcoded,
and distributed worldwide

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:44.030
to hundreds of millions
of people via YouTube.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:46.420
So to be able to offer
rendering solutions on top

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.230
of this infrastructure
is really exciting.

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:49.646
And I'm going to
talk about what I

00:01:49.646 --> 00:01:51.640
believe as the scalability
and the visions

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:53.537
you can achieve from this.

00:01:53.537 --> 00:01:55.120
And certainly as
[? Belle ?] addressed

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.950
before, rendering is only one
of the workloads we're tackling.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:02.170
And we're doing this by
working with our own technology

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:04.830
as well as collaborating
with best of breed.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.990
One of the things that when I
joined Google wanted to make

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:11.300
very clear is that we are not
going for a one-size-fits-all

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:11.890
solution.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:15.040
In the media space, every studio
has a different way of working,

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.494
has a different pipeline,
has different tools

00:02:17.494 --> 00:02:18.910
that they're used
to working with.

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:21.530
So we've spent a
lot of time not only

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.330
working on our own
internal technology

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:26.957
but really outreaching to
the best-of-breed technology

00:02:26.957 --> 00:02:27.790
providers out there.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:30.020
And you'll see some of them
speaking later on today

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:33.010
as to how they've been able to
bring their solutions to Google

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.400
Cloud Platform to
allow us to achieve

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.060
more and more offerings.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:40.840
As a rendering
platform in particular,

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:44.000
there are some amazing things
that we can do with Google,

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:45.719
such as the permanent billing.

00:02:45.719 --> 00:02:47.260
Again, [? as Belle ?]
was mentioning,

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:49.560
you're only paying
for what you use.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.410
There's no 60-minute blocks.

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:53.660
There's no building
out of infrastructure

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:54.690
when you don't need it.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:56.890
So especially for
burst capacity,

00:02:56.890 --> 00:02:59.110
it's a fantastic tool.

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:03.080
And along those lines, it's
also designed to save you

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.952
money the more hours you run.

00:03:04.952 --> 00:03:07.160
So we have this thing called
sustained use discounts.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:09.740
Again, it doesn't require
any sort of pre-purchase

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:13.270
of allocated compute hours.

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:15.760
Literally, the more you run
a machine during the month,

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:16.580
the less you pay.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:18.310
It just naturally gets cheaper.

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:21.440
And that's how we feel
it should be at Google.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:22.700
Huge capacity.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:23.880
You'll hear from Rodeo FX.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.338
You'll hear from
Atomic Fiction how

00:03:25.338 --> 00:03:27.520
they've been able to
scale to tens of thousands

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:30.172
of cores literally at the
press a button without having

00:03:30.172 --> 00:03:32.380
to reserve these, with having
them available and just

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:33.450
paying for what you use.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:36.490
And you can spin these
up very, very quickly.

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:40.260
As you'll see in a Zync demo
I'll give it a little bit,

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:43.010
I'm able to spin up 60 nodes
in a matter of minutes,

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:45.700
get the job up and running, and
finish the job, spin it down

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:46.730
when it's done.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:48.590
And then specific to
some of the rendering

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:50.995
challenges we're tackling,
we have our turnkey solution,

00:03:50.995 --> 00:03:54.090
Zync Render, which I'll
show you at the end, as well

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:55.760
as these technology
collaborations.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:57.670
So I really can't
emphasize how important

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:00.080
it is for us to
build that ecosystem

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:01.830
working with companies
like Thinkbox,

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:04.860
with Avere, with Atomic
Fiction and their Conductor IO

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:07.780
rendering solution to
give you as many options

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.980
as you need to most
seamlessly and securely

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:15.460
migrate your existing pipeline
from an on-premise workload

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:18.700
to Google Cloud Platform.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:20.170
So looking at
rendering workloads,

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:23.730
whether you're doing an NFS
mount on a nearby data center

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.841
or doing an asset sync
to a remote data center,

00:04:26.841 --> 00:04:28.465
there are a lot of
the same challenges,

00:04:28.465 --> 00:04:31.080
a lot of the same things
you have to build.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:35.910
But what I really look at it
as is a hierarchy of needs.

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:38.240
From the bottom up,
at the baseline,

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:41.820
you have to make sure you can
get your assets to the cloud.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:43.919
And you also want to
be smart about it.

00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:45.960
You don't want to mirror
your entire file system.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:48.799
You want to be able to parse a
scene for all its dependencies.

00:04:48.799 --> 00:04:50.840
You want to make sure
you're only ever pushing up

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:52.502
the assets that are needed.

00:04:52.502 --> 00:04:54.460
You want to make sure
you have enough licenses.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.422
So you need the value--
if you're spinning up

00:04:56.422 --> 00:04:58.630
1,000 machines, you want to
make sure you can spin up

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:02.084
1,000 concurrent licenses as
well and only pay for those.

00:05:02.084 --> 00:05:03.750
And at the top of
this pyramid, I really

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:05.100
see pipeline integration.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.670
So yeah, I can get
all the data up there,

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:11.320
I can intelligently parse a
scene, I can get licenses.

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:13.720
But what we're really striving
for with our solutions

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:15.280
on Google Cloud
Platform is something

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:17.310
that I keep saying fits
within your workflow,

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:18.870
makes it feel seamless.

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:21.552
So to the artists
or the TD, there's

00:05:21.552 --> 00:05:23.760
no going outside of the way
they would normally work.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:24.801
There are no extra steps.

00:05:24.801 --> 00:05:26.940
And it just feels
exactly like it's part

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:28.450
of your local infrastructure.

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:31.350
The way I envision cloud
rendering essentially

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:34.140
is that you could have
10,000 machines running

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:36.046
on Google Cloud Platform
at a data center,

00:05:36.046 --> 00:05:38.420
but it should feel like it's
in the room next door to you

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:41.610
in terms of the responsiveness,
in terms of the interactivity,

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:43.010
and in terms of the integration.

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:47.880
So one of the interesting
things that I've come across

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:50.180
in talking to a lot
of studios and getting

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:53.520
their feedback as to
how they view rendering

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:55.490
is the total cost of ownership.

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:58.600
And for a lot of studios, it's
simply along the lines of,

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:01.150
well, yes, I've invested
this much in a render farm.

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:02.650
These are my capital expenses.

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:05.730
And these are my ongoing
operating expenses

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:09.530
for like HVAC, licenses, power.

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:12.540
What I think we can do
with Google Cloud Platform,

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:14.800
and we think we've
seen done with quite

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:18.160
a few of our customers
so far, is also

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.370
cut down on the render times,
give you burst capacity

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:25.790
to the fact where the unknown,
unmonitored cost of idle artist

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:30.160
time, which a lot of studios
don't take into account,

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:31.764
is now really severely impacted.

00:06:31.764 --> 00:06:33.930
So traditionally, you're
at a visual effects studio,

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:36.346
you have somewhat of a limited
capacity for a render farm.

00:06:36.346 --> 00:06:39.040
So at some point, you're paying
a CG artist whatever you're

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:41.960
paying them to sit around and
wait for renders to come back

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:43.377
while they're in the queue.

00:06:43.377 --> 00:06:45.210
So what we can do with
Google Cloud Platform

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:47.940
is achieve that scalability
where that added cost

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.650
that maybe you're not
factoring into what

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:53.780
the daily operational
expenses of a render farm are,

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:55.300
we actually are
mitigating those.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.800
And you're not having artists
sitting around anymore waiting

00:06:57.800 --> 00:06:59.290
for renders to come back.

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:01.960
But there's some amazing
parallel processing,

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:04.210
or jobs are coming back to
you really, really quickly,

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:07.540
and the artist efficiency,
which might be 20% or 30%

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:10.600
or 40% wasted time, is
really maximized so they're

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:11.880
able to work all the time.

00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:16.040
As I was mentioning
before, we're

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:19.080
collaborating with what we
feel are the leading partners,

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:22.430
the leading technology providers
in the visual effects animation

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:23.460
software industry.

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:25.740
With Zync Render, we
are providing licenses

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:28.440
from Chaos Group, The
Foundry, and Solid Angle.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:30.690
But as I also mentioned
before, we realize Zync is not

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:31.980
a one-size-fits-all solution.

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:34.040
It's great for small
to medium shops.

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:35.850
But we want to make
sure we can work across

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:37.970
as many pipelines
as possible, handle

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:39.559
as many workloads as possible.

00:07:39.559 --> 00:07:41.850
So from that, we are really
excited to be working again

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:44.607
with very industry-leading
companies such as Avere,

00:07:44.607 --> 00:07:46.940
and their virtual filer is
now available on Google Cloud

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:47.800
Platform.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:49.370
Atomic Fiction,
which for a long time

00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:50.630
was actually a
Zync customer, has

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:52.390
built their own
fantastic cloud rendering

00:07:52.390 --> 00:07:55.270
platform called Conductor
IO on Google Cloud Platform.

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:57.910
And you'll hear about them
as well later on today.

00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:01.320
And Thinbox Software, probably
the leading render manager

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:03.570
with their Deadline
solution, has also now

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:05.780
seamlessly extended
their platform

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:06.990
onto Google Cloud Platform.

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:08.770
And you'll be hearing
from them as well.

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:11.300
So just to reiterate,
the way I see

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:14.190
this is it's all about all the
technology collaborations we

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:16.920
can do and how we can bring all
these people to Google Cloud

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:20.680
Platform to migrate
your workload.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:22.980
Avere in particular I want
to call out, as we just

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:24.060
announced this at NAB.

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:27.090
And this is really
designed for large studios.

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:29.940
Studios that are using Avere
locally to cache their data

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:32.380
can now seamlessly
migrate and sync that

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:34.309
to Google Cloud Platform.

00:08:34.309 --> 00:08:37.500
So going back the idea of a
hybrid environment, if you have

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:39.960
studios in multiple locations
with multiple Averes,

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:41.350
all of that now
can be seamlessly

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:43.070
passed back and forth.

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:46.360
And we are focused on bringing
this hybrid environment

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:47.200
to studios.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:50.470
We understand large studios have
a lot of on-premise hardware

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:52.310
they've invested in over time.

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:54.100
But rather than buy
more hardware or rent

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:56.240
hardware for burst capacity,
you can now literally

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:00.060
extend at the push of a button
to tens of thousands of cores

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:02.390
and sync all of your hot
data from your local Avere

00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:05.955
caches to our Google Cloud
Platform Avere cache,

00:09:05.955 --> 00:09:07.330
which is really,
really exciting.

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:10.860
And you'll be hearing
from them as well.

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:13.430
Some of the early customer
successes we've had, again,

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:16.270
Atomic Fiction, a pioneer
in visual effects rendering

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:16.934
on the cloud.

00:09:16.934 --> 00:09:18.350
They've been doing
it for a while.

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:19.870
They'll tell you about
some of the great things

00:09:19.870 --> 00:09:20.779
they're working on.

00:09:20.779 --> 00:09:23.320
Rodeo Effects, you'll be hearing
from Jordan Soles, their CTO

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:25.032
and executive producer,
later on today

00:09:25.032 --> 00:09:27.240
about how they've been able
to integrate Google Cloud

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:28.480
Platform and burst.

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:30.590
Industriromantik,
a smaller studio

00:09:30.590 --> 00:09:32.470
you might not know
about, they're in Sweden,

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:34.810
an architectural design
visualization firm.

00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:36.980
But again, being able
to seamlessly integrate

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:39.290
their pipeline into
Google Cloud Platform,

00:09:39.290 --> 00:09:40.920
showing the breadth
of solutions we're

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:44.240
able to offer from small studios
all the way up to the really

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:46.970
large ones such
as Framestore, who

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:49.630
did a blog post in the fall
about how they were able to use

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:55.380
Google Cloud Platform to burst
to 15,000 cores for a very

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:56.060
short time.

00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:58.040
So the alternative
is literally for them

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:01.360
spending $300,000
on new hardware

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:03.410
for this very short burst
capacity, which really

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:06.370
doesn't make a lot of financial
sense and is a lot of work

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:07.450
to get up and running.

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:09.420
And with Google Cloud
Platform, they're

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:12.852
actually able to build
their VM image within a day,

00:10:12.852 --> 00:10:15.310
get it up and running, deploy
it, and start rendering tests

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:15.860
the next day.

00:10:18.417 --> 00:10:20.000
I do want to take a
little bit of time

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:22.460
to talk to you about
Zync specifically.

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:24.970
It's very near and dear to
me, and it's a part of Google.

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:27.600
So I wanted to spend a
little bit more time talking

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:30.020
about Google's own cloud
rendering solution in addition

00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:33.050
to all of these great technology
collaborations we have.

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:36.600
So Zync itself is built from
the ground up as a cloud

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:37.650
rendering solution.

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:41.910
It started in 2010 at a
very small visual effects

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:44.460
studio called Zero
Visual Effects in Boston.

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.839
The founder came from a long
career at Digital Domain,

00:10:46.839 --> 00:10:48.880
I think actually worked
across the street, if not

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:50.980
in one of these buildings,
for about 10 years,

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:54.290
and wanted to build an
artist-friendly solution,

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:56.970
something that obfuscated
cloud, so for the artists

00:10:56.970 --> 00:10:59.570
they didn't have to worry about
setting up filers or getting

00:10:59.570 --> 00:11:02.960
licenses or command line
syncing any of their assets,

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:04.910
but literally a
front-end UI that would

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:08.160
be easy to use and fit into
their existing pipeline.

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:10.200
So the origins of Zync
and the genesis of Zync

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:12.980
has always been from a
professional visual effects

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:14.170
background.

00:11:14.170 --> 00:11:16.470
We like to say it's by
artists for artists.

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:18.310
We understand how people work.

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:19.880
We understand how
they want to work.

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:22.700
And we have done our best
to integrate our solution

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:24.890
into that existing
pipeline to make it

00:11:24.890 --> 00:11:26.980
as easy as possible to
use cloud, going back

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:31.630
to the idea of having a local
render farm in your back which

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:37.296
is actually thousands of
miles away in a data center.

00:11:37.296 --> 00:11:40.460
Had a lot of great success
with feature films that

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:41.780
have been rendered on Zync.

00:11:41.780 --> 00:11:44.990
Beyond that, we're looking
at small to medium studios,

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:49.150
over 12 million core hours in
total, hundreds of commercials.

00:11:49.150 --> 00:11:51.760
In fact, Moonbot Studios
in Shreveport, Louisiana,

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:54.230
just won two Emmys for
their short film "Silent."

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:56.720
It was done for
Adobe Labs last year.

00:11:56.720 --> 00:12:00.850
They rendered the entire thing
on Zync about nine months ago.

00:12:00.850 --> 00:12:03.200
A classic example
of a small studio

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:04.902
that had a lot of
creative talent,

00:12:04.902 --> 00:12:07.110
didn't necessarily want to
go out and spend the money

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:09.900
or could afford to spend
the money on a large render

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:12.210
farm for this for 4K
project-- it was the first 4K

00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:14.459
project they've been given
for this three-minute short

00:12:14.459 --> 00:12:17.720
animated film-- but were really
able to focus on the creative

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:20.107
by using Zync as an
integrated solution that

00:12:20.107 --> 00:12:22.440
fit within their pipeline,
let the artists do their work

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:27.060
and submit the renders
from Maya as they needed.

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:30.400
Our workflow, and I'll
show you in a moment,

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:33.560
is really all focused
about intelligent asset

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:35.270
management and file management.

00:12:35.270 --> 00:12:36.940
I think in our
industry we all realize

00:12:36.940 --> 00:12:38.520
we create a lot of data.

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:41.385
We're moving a lot of data
between different locations.

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:46.310
And it's no
different with cloud.

00:12:46.310 --> 00:12:48.760
You have to push data
up at some point.

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:52.200
So the way we've designed
Zync is to actually analyze

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:54.960
a scene for all of the assets,
all of the dependencies,

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:57.620
and upload those ahead of
time in the background.

00:12:57.620 --> 00:12:59.500
So being able to
manage your pipeline,

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:01.580
being smart about
pushing all of the assets

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:03.610
up to the cloud
so they're there,

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:06.750
and at render time itself
when you go to submit a job,

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:09.350
the only thing that's going
to go up is a scene file.

00:13:09.350 --> 00:13:10.880
We also keep a copy
of anything you

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:13.260
render on Zync on the cloud.

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:15.920
So if you're pushing
those CG renders into comp

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:18.820
and you're compositing in NUKE--
and again, I'll show you this--

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:20.770
and then you go ahead
and launch a NUKE job,

00:13:20.770 --> 00:13:22.380
there's no file I/O to be done.

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:24.130
Everything has been
rendered on the cloud.

00:13:24.130 --> 00:13:25.260
It stays on the cloud.

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:26.930
So we've tried to
design a system that

00:13:26.930 --> 00:13:30.042
minimizes the
bottlenecks of file I/O

00:13:30.042 --> 00:13:31.250
as it stands in our industry.

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:33.250
And I think we've done a
pretty good job with it.

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:34.160
So with that, I'm
going to actually take

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:36.800
a few minutes to just give
you a walkthrough of Zync here

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:40.390
running on Google
Cloud Platform and show

00:13:40.390 --> 00:13:41.740
you the reality of things.

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:45.200
So Zync is now in a beta phase.

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:46.720
And I've got a Maya scene here.

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:49.290
So all you have seen Maya.

00:13:49.290 --> 00:13:53.490
So this is just a
60-frame sequence here.

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:56.310
This police car actually has
about 500 different textures.

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:57.300
There's HDR material.

00:13:57.300 --> 00:13:58.400
There's dependencies.

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:00.132
So there are a lot
of assets to manage.

00:14:00.132 --> 00:14:02.090
And as I mentioned at
the beginning of my talk,

00:14:02.090 --> 00:14:04.131
you don't want to mirror
your entire file system.

00:14:04.131 --> 00:14:05.900
You want to be
intelligent about actually

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:08.150
parsing only the data you need.

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:09.880
So what we're able
to do with Zync

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:12.080
is actually parse a scene
file for all of that data

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:13.560
and upload it ahead of time.

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:16.350
So I want to go ahead and
launch the Zync plugin

00:14:16.350 --> 00:14:17.750
from the Zync Shelf in Maya.

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:19.250
So we have a plugin
inside of Maya.

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:22.320
And once I launch this, you can
actually see our dialog box.

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:26.227
So the way we manage file I/O
is to run this upload-only job.

00:14:26.227 --> 00:14:28.310
So when I click on this,
everything is grayed out.

00:14:28.310 --> 00:14:30.476
I would go ahead and put
my username and password in

00:14:30.476 --> 00:14:31.307
and submit the job.

00:14:31.307 --> 00:14:33.890
So at that point, Zync is going
to do a scene analysis, again,

00:14:33.890 --> 00:14:35.950
pull all my dependencies,
all of my assets

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:37.520
and upload those
in the background.

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:39.940
So by the time it actually
comes to render a job,

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:41.120
everything is up there.

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:42.505
So I've already done this.

00:14:42.505 --> 00:14:44.130
So for the purposes
of the demo, I just

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:46.004
want to really quickly
run a job and walk you

00:14:46.004 --> 00:14:49.000
through the user interface
and what the workflow is like.

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:51.210
So you can see here, I've
got the number of machines

00:14:51.210 --> 00:14:52.540
I can choose to render.

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:54.621
And these are the Google
Cloud Platform machines.

00:14:54.621 --> 00:14:56.870
So you don't have to go out
and build any sort of VMs.

00:14:56.870 --> 00:14:59.730
You don't have to configure
any sort of command line render

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:00.320
management.

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:02.260
And this is a 60-frame
sequence, so Zync

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:05.090
has analyzed all of the data
necessary for a Maya scene.

00:15:05.090 --> 00:15:08.000
It's a 60-frame sequence, so I
just want to use 60 machines.

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:09.745
This idea burst
ability, of scalability,

00:15:09.745 --> 00:15:11.120
of being able to
turn jobs around

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:13.607
faster so your artists are
not sitting around idle at all

00:15:13.607 --> 00:15:15.940
is something that's really
important to us and something

00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:18.214
we're working very
hard to deliver.

00:15:18.214 --> 00:15:20.380
So machine types, I've got
several different machine

00:15:20.380 --> 00:15:20.650
types.

00:15:20.650 --> 00:15:23.025
And these are based on the
standard Google Cloud Platform

00:15:23.025 --> 00:15:23.560
machines.

00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:26.660
I'm going to use the
32-core machines.

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:29.220
And you can see that's
priced at $2.08 an hour.

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:31.770
My total cost is $124.80.

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:34.280
But that's for the hour,
and we bill by the minute.

00:15:34.280 --> 00:15:35.400
So if you were to
render this and it's

00:15:35.400 --> 00:15:36.774
taking 11 minutes
in total, you'd

00:15:36.774 --> 00:15:39.470
only pay that percentage
of the entire estimated

00:15:39.470 --> 00:15:40.220
cost for the hour.

00:15:40.220 --> 00:15:44.770
So I think having
permanent billing

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:46.950
is really critical in
the rendering workspace.

00:15:46.950 --> 00:15:48.840
You're not wasting any time.

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:50.850
I'll go ahead and
set my project.

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:52.045
It's already synced.

00:15:52.045 --> 00:15:54.670
I'll parse my Maya scene for my
output directory for my project

00:15:54.670 --> 00:15:55.390
folder.

00:15:55.390 --> 00:15:56.590
It knows I'm using V-Ray.

00:15:56.590 --> 00:15:59.420
So for the renders, we support
V-Ray and Arnold right now.

00:15:59.420 --> 00:16:02.300
For V-Ray, we support nightlies
and distributed rendering.

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:03.770
I'll just set the
chunk size to 1,

00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:06.160
so I'll have every machine
working on a frame.

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:08.980
It's going to give me all
the different camera options.

00:16:08.980 --> 00:16:16.920
I'll choose my beauty layer and
put in my username and password

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:18.612
and submit the job.

00:16:18.612 --> 00:16:20.570
So Zync is then going to
go through, do a pass,

00:16:20.570 --> 00:16:22.560
analyze the scene,
and actually upload

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:25.617
the scene to Google Cloud
Platform and start rendering.

00:16:25.617 --> 00:16:27.200
So we'll just give
this a minute here.

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:30.130
It's going to go through
everything here, generate

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:32.760
that output pass, and upload it.

00:16:35.937 --> 00:16:37.520
And so imagine you
have an artist now,

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:40.100
and they have 20
shots to work on,

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:42.426
and you have a render
queue that's backed up.

00:16:42.426 --> 00:16:44.300
You'd essentially be
waiting for the one shot

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:45.570
at a time to just submit them.

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:47.870
But if you have an artist
who's got 20 shots lined up

00:16:47.870 --> 00:16:49.650
and you're using Google Cloud
Platform and you're using Zync,

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:51.380
you can just submit those
jobs one after another.

00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:52.290
There is no queue.

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:56.480
Just do one machine
in parallel per frame.

00:16:56.480 --> 00:17:00.700
So the job has been
submitted to Zync.

00:17:00.700 --> 00:17:03.750
I can go ahead and move
to my Web Render Manager.

00:17:03.750 --> 00:17:06.680
So this is something we
built from the ground up.

00:17:06.680 --> 00:17:09.260
It looks like many of the
render queue managers out there.

00:17:09.260 --> 00:17:11.440
Again, we've tried to
design this with the idea

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:13.551
that people are using
existing software tools.

00:17:13.551 --> 00:17:15.550
They want something that
fits in their pipeline.

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:17.530
They want something
that's easily integrated.

00:17:17.530 --> 00:17:19.613
And in a few minutes, the
job is going to start up

00:17:19.613 --> 00:17:21.269
and we'll see all
60 of those VMs

00:17:21.269 --> 00:17:24.040
on Google Cloud Platform
with V-Ray licenses

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:26.060
spinning up and
rendering the job.

00:17:26.060 --> 00:17:28.230
I think one other
important thing to note

00:17:28.230 --> 00:17:32.130
is that this is not only
a GUI-based application,

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:34.160
but we also have full
Python scripting.

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:35.650
All of our plugins
are open source.

00:17:35.650 --> 00:17:36.580
They're available on GitHub.

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:38.413
So we've had studios--
in fact, some of whom

00:17:38.413 --> 00:17:41.050
are here-- who have done their
own command line scripting

00:17:41.050 --> 00:17:43.300
if it's integrated into their
asset management system,

00:17:43.300 --> 00:17:47.190
into their render manager to do
more advanced types of render

00:17:47.190 --> 00:17:48.040
control management.

00:17:48.040 --> 00:17:51.290
So we're trying to accommodate
as many different workflows

00:17:51.290 --> 00:17:54.410
as possible and as many
different pipelines as possible

00:17:54.410 --> 00:17:55.139
within Zync.

00:17:55.139 --> 00:17:57.680
Again, going from the fact that
we come from a visual effects

00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:00.380
background, we understand
how artists work.

00:18:00.380 --> 00:18:02.100
So this job is now
spinning up here.

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:04.224
In a minute, we'll start
to see all of my machines.

00:18:04.224 --> 00:18:06.930
But you can see I've got all
of my frames queued up here.

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:08.110
I'll see all 60 frames.

00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:09.790
They're all queued
up ready to go.

00:18:09.790 --> 00:18:14.371
You can see my output directory,
my frame range, my render

00:18:14.371 --> 00:18:14.870
output.

00:18:14.870 --> 00:18:16.950
So this is essentially
my output directory here.

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:18.620
This is my beauty pass.

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:20.430
So this is empty right now.

00:18:20.430 --> 00:18:22.719
But as this job finishes,
those EXR frames

00:18:22.719 --> 00:18:24.260
are going to come
back one at a time.

00:18:24.260 --> 00:18:26.430
So you're not waiting for
an entire job to finish.

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:29.420
You're only waiting as long as
it takes for each chunk to go.

00:18:29.420 --> 00:18:31.320
And in this case, I've
set the chunk to 1.

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:33.970
So very quickly, we'll start
to see those frames spin up,

00:18:33.970 --> 00:18:37.777
render, and come right back down
to my local output directory.

00:18:37.777 --> 00:18:40.110
So as an artist, you don't
have to think about anything.

00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:41.340
You submit the job.

00:18:41.340 --> 00:18:42.072
The job renders.

00:18:42.072 --> 00:18:44.030
It comes right back down
to where you expected.

00:18:44.030 --> 00:18:45.620
There's no fetching of files.

00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:48.870
And it's designed as a
seamless integration.

00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:50.920
So we've got all these
machines now spinning up.

00:18:50.920 --> 00:18:53.290
You can see, I spoke at
the beginning about VMs

00:18:53.290 --> 00:18:56.265
spinning up very, very quickly
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:18:56.265 --> 00:18:57.140
This is no exception.

00:18:57.140 --> 00:18:59.810
I've already got 34 of the
60 machines I requested up

00:18:59.810 --> 00:19:01.880
and running in a
matter of minutes.

00:19:01.880 --> 00:19:05.039
So we'll soon see all 60 of
those machines come online.

00:19:05.039 --> 00:19:06.330
We'll see everything rendering.

00:19:06.330 --> 00:19:08.529
And we'll see the final output.

00:19:08.529 --> 00:19:11.070
So while this actually starts
up, there are a few more things

00:19:11.070 --> 00:19:13.340
I want to show you
behind the scenes

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:16.570
that we built within Zync to
give you pipeline integration.

00:19:16.570 --> 00:19:19.600
So I have a Maya
account page here.

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:21.870
And from this, this is all
the administrative stuff,

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:24.590
all of the custom scripts,
all of the shaders, anything

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:26.620
you would want to add
within this workflow

00:19:26.620 --> 00:19:27.940
is available through Zync.

00:19:27.940 --> 00:19:30.830
So we have the Admin Account
tab at the beginning.

00:19:30.830 --> 00:19:32.620
And this where you
can set your username,

00:19:32.620 --> 00:19:35.476
your password, your email.

00:19:35.476 --> 00:19:37.850
If you're a non-admin, this
is the only thing you'll see.

00:19:37.850 --> 00:19:39.340
So if you're just an
artist using the system,

00:19:39.340 --> 00:19:40.855
this is the only
access you'll have.

00:19:40.855 --> 00:19:42.980
But then we've also got
some advanced configuration

00:19:42.980 --> 00:19:43.940
controls.

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:48.500
So you can configure things like
the max number of running jobs,

00:19:48.500 --> 00:19:49.890
your custom gizmos for NUKE.

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:52.525
If you have custom shaders for
Maya, we support all of that.

00:19:52.525 --> 00:19:54.650
Drive mapping-- so let's
say not all of your assets

00:19:54.650 --> 00:19:56.830
are living on a single
NFS, you have assets living

00:19:56.830 --> 00:19:58.663
on different machines,
you're able to set up

00:19:58.663 --> 00:20:02.660
drive mapping to make sure you
can access all of those assets.

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:04.680
You're also able to
monitor your usage

00:20:04.680 --> 00:20:08.470
on a monthly, daily, or weekly
basis, by host application,

00:20:08.470 --> 00:20:09.067
by job.

00:20:09.067 --> 00:20:11.650
So all of the billing you get
it done through the Google Cloud

00:20:11.650 --> 00:20:13.190
Platform billing console.

00:20:13.190 --> 00:20:15.929
And you actually get a
breakdown for every job ID

00:20:15.929 --> 00:20:17.220
and how much you spent on every

00:20:17.220 --> 00:20:17.719
One.

00:20:17.719 --> 00:20:19.770
So I could say, yes,
I've rendered 45 hours

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:22.140
of the 16-core V-Ray
for this job for Nissan,

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:27.650
or 300 hours at $2.00 an hour
of Arnold for this job for Nike.

00:20:27.650 --> 00:20:30.130
So it's very intuitive
in terms of being

00:20:30.130 --> 00:20:32.190
able to take those
spends on rendering

00:20:32.190 --> 00:20:33.960
and actually quantify
to the client,

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:35.684
yes, this is how
much it costs you.

00:20:35.684 --> 00:20:37.100
These are the
iterations you want.

00:20:37.100 --> 00:20:40.902
This is actually how much you
paid for it down to the minute.

00:20:40.902 --> 00:20:42.610
And I should note that
all of our billing

00:20:42.610 --> 00:20:45.080
is done totally inclusive.

00:20:45.080 --> 00:20:47.450
So as I showed you
back in the Maya plugin

00:20:47.450 --> 00:20:51.990
here, when I launched
a job, this price

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:54.260
you can see on the
32-core is $2.08 an hour.

00:20:54.260 --> 00:20:56.310
That's inclusive
for V-Ray as well.

00:20:56.310 --> 00:20:58.700
That's not a separate
breakout of compute,

00:20:58.700 --> 00:21:01.170
then you have to pay for Zync
infrastructure or anything

00:21:01.170 --> 00:21:02.003
like that, or V-Ray.

00:21:02.003 --> 00:21:05.630
It's all bundled, trying to make
this as easy and as friendly

00:21:05.630 --> 00:21:08.150
as possible.

00:21:08.150 --> 00:21:09.510
A few more tabs here.

00:21:09.510 --> 00:21:12.120
You can add users, projects.

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:14.760
So all of the data you render
on Zync is stored on Zync.

00:21:14.760 --> 00:21:17.520
And I'll show you a nice
example of that in a moment.

00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:19.830
But you also have full
access to all of your data.

00:21:19.830 --> 00:21:21.250
You can delete that any time.

00:21:21.250 --> 00:21:23.071
Typically, data
storage is cheap.

00:21:23.071 --> 00:21:24.570
You're not paying
much on your bill.

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:27.790
It's $0.20 a gig a month, and
that ends up being maybe 5%

00:21:27.790 --> 00:21:29.770
of a user's bill versus
the total compute.

00:21:29.770 --> 00:21:32.290
But you're able to
see all of the usage

00:21:32.290 --> 00:21:33.380
on a per-project basis.

00:21:33.380 --> 00:21:34.810
And you can delete
that any time.

00:21:34.810 --> 00:21:36.430
So typically, after
a job is done,

00:21:36.430 --> 00:21:39.450
and perhaps you want to archive
it off to our nearline storage

00:21:39.450 --> 00:21:42.235
or you're doing LTO, you're
able to go ahead and delete

00:21:42.235 --> 00:21:43.610
those projects
whenever you want.

00:21:43.610 --> 00:21:44.984
And one last thing
to mention is,

00:21:44.984 --> 00:21:47.860
we've mirrored the entire file
structure of the files that

00:21:47.860 --> 00:21:50.420
have been uploaded from
your local environment

00:21:50.420 --> 00:21:51.650
onto Google Cloud Platform.

00:21:51.650 --> 00:21:55.760
So if I were to look
at my demo project

00:21:55.760 --> 00:21:59.634
here, just very quickly, you can
see I've got all of my scenes,

00:21:59.634 --> 00:22:01.300
all of my source
images, all of the data

00:22:01.300 --> 00:22:03.240
that goes into making
this Maya scene.

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:05.750
So when I go up to the cloud,
to Google Cloud Platform,

00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:09.380
and I'm looking at the storage
we built for you of a filer

00:22:09.380 --> 00:22:11.670
on top of Google Cloud
Platform, I can go through here

00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:13.870
and I can actually see a mirror
of what my local directory

00:22:13.870 --> 00:22:14.411
structure is.

00:22:14.411 --> 00:22:17.037
So everything is meant to
be as intuitive as possible,

00:22:17.037 --> 00:22:18.120
as simplistic as possible.

00:22:18.120 --> 00:22:20.010
So I can go back
into my demo folder,

00:22:20.010 --> 00:22:23.250
I can see all my Maya scenes,
I can see all the assets,

00:22:23.250 --> 00:22:26.240
and I'll be able to actually
parse all of the data.

00:22:26.240 --> 00:22:28.472
So you can see scenes,
images, source images.

00:22:28.472 --> 00:22:30.680
So you're able to actually
see all of the data that's

00:22:30.680 --> 00:22:34.330
needed for the render that's
been uploaded to Google Cloud

00:22:34.330 --> 00:22:35.435
Platform, to Zync.

00:22:35.435 --> 00:22:37.060
So when I go back to
my main page here,

00:22:37.060 --> 00:22:38.810
we've actually got the
job up and running.

00:22:38.810 --> 00:22:40.862
And you can already see
the first few frames are

00:22:40.862 --> 00:22:42.070
going to be done in a moment.

00:22:42.070 --> 00:22:43.980
So this first frame is at 99%.

00:22:43.980 --> 00:22:48.990
And it'll go from rendering
to downloading to done.

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:51.630
And I'm just going to leave
my file directory open,

00:22:51.630 --> 00:22:55.822
so you'll see those EXR
frames come in one at a time.

00:22:55.822 --> 00:22:57.780
But within this, we've
also done the same thing

00:22:57.780 --> 00:22:59.250
with our log files.

00:22:59.250 --> 00:23:01.677
You're able to go ahead and
view all of your V-Ray logs

00:23:01.677 --> 00:23:03.260
or your Arnold logs
or your NUKE logs.

00:23:03.260 --> 00:23:05.801
Everything's been repacked from
a cloud environment to local.

00:23:05.801 --> 00:23:07.310
So everything you're
seeing, again,

00:23:07.310 --> 00:23:09.192
with the feel of a
local render farm.

00:23:09.192 --> 00:23:10.650
There's nothing
cloud that you have

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:12.360
to worry about
pathing differently

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:13.506
or anything like that.

00:23:13.506 --> 00:23:15.130
So this first frame
is now downloading.

00:23:15.130 --> 00:23:16.350
You can see it's 100% done.

00:23:16.350 --> 00:23:19.030
And it's popping right back
into my local output directory.

00:23:19.030 --> 00:23:21.430
So I can actually see
the output of that

00:23:21.430 --> 00:23:25.340
very quickly without having
to spend a lot of time waiting

00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:27.140
for the whole job to finish.

00:23:27.140 --> 00:23:30.066
A few other things to mention,
just very briefly here,

00:23:30.066 --> 00:23:31.940
that you have all the
standard render manager

00:23:31.940 --> 00:23:34.140
controls you have in a job.

00:23:34.140 --> 00:23:34.995
You can stop a job.

00:23:34.995 --> 00:23:35.965
You can restart a job.

00:23:35.965 --> 00:23:37.880
You can pause a
job, cancel a job.

00:23:37.880 --> 00:23:40.734
You can add more machines to
it by changing the job options.

00:23:40.734 --> 00:23:42.400
Zync's already rendered
this job before,

00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:43.750
so it knows how long
the job should take.

00:23:43.750 --> 00:23:45.050
And it's giving me
an approximation

00:23:45.050 --> 00:23:46.341
of how much of the job is done.

00:23:46.341 --> 00:23:49.200
You can already see it's
48% of the way done.

00:23:49.200 --> 00:23:51.245
And that'll continue
to populate.

00:23:51.245 --> 00:23:54.210
And on a per-task basis, if you
ever want to take a single VM

00:23:54.210 --> 00:23:57.055
and add controls to
that, you can click on it

00:23:57.055 --> 00:23:58.930
and then you've got all
these tasks controls.

00:23:58.930 --> 00:24:01.840
So I can requeue the
task, skip the task,

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:02.979
and so on and so forth.

00:24:02.979 --> 00:24:04.520
So having this is
integrated solution

00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:06.430
is really, really
important for us.

00:24:06.430 --> 00:24:08.460
So you can see these
first few frames are done.

00:24:08.460 --> 00:24:10.543
I'm not going to wait for
the whole job to finish.

00:24:10.543 --> 00:24:13.340
But again, I've got my EXRs
coming down one at a time.

00:24:13.340 --> 00:24:15.810
And that idea of
parallel scalability,

00:24:15.810 --> 00:24:17.690
I don't have to have
an artist submitting

00:24:17.690 --> 00:24:20.370
a job to a limited farm
and waiting for that job

00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:22.700
and waiting for the jobs
queued behind it to finish.

00:24:22.700 --> 00:24:24.554
I spin up one VM
per frame, the job

00:24:24.554 --> 00:24:26.470
gets done as quickly as
possible, and I'm back

00:24:26.470 --> 00:24:27.470
and I'm working with it.

00:24:27.470 --> 00:24:29.590
So my EXRs are coming
in, and I essentially

00:24:29.590 --> 00:24:31.580
want to move to the next
stage in the pipeline,

00:24:31.580 --> 00:24:32.714
to compositing.

00:24:32.714 --> 00:24:34.380
So as you can see
here, all those frames

00:24:34.380 --> 00:24:35.720
are coming back down locally.

00:24:35.720 --> 00:24:39.560
We're also keeping a
copy on cloud storage.

00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:42.020
So when I go into NUKE, I
have a background plate here.

00:24:42.020 --> 00:24:43.920
This is actually
rendered on Zync.

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:46.090
But I have my police car
that I just finished,

00:24:46.090 --> 00:24:48.180
and it's also been
rendered on Zync.

00:24:48.180 --> 00:24:50.140
But I have loaded
up the local EXR,

00:24:50.140 --> 00:24:52.480
just did a very
quick A over B comp,

00:24:52.480 --> 00:24:55.690
a little bit of motion
blur, a little bit of glow.

00:24:55.690 --> 00:24:58.090
Save that project,
and I can essentially

00:24:58.090 --> 00:25:00.170
just repeat the same process.

00:25:00.170 --> 00:25:03.661
So now when I go to
render on Zync here,

00:25:03.661 --> 00:25:04.910
the plugin is going to launch.

00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:06.860
And you can see, I have
this Upload Only check box.

00:25:06.860 --> 00:25:08.800
So this is what you want to
do with live action footage.

00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:10.810
You get the plates at
the beginning of a show,

00:25:10.810 --> 00:25:12.810
just bring in all your
plates into a dummy comp,

00:25:12.810 --> 00:25:14.720
run upload only, submit the job.

00:25:14.720 --> 00:25:17.861
But in this case, I know this
CG was just rendered on Zync,

00:25:17.861 --> 00:25:18.610
and Zync knows it.

00:25:18.610 --> 00:25:20.776
Zync's going to make sure
that all the assets needed

00:25:20.776 --> 00:25:21.910
are already there.

00:25:21.910 --> 00:25:24.070
In this case, that's the case.

00:25:24.070 --> 00:25:26.720
So I can just choose
a 16-core machine.

00:25:26.720 --> 00:25:35.020
I'll just use one machine, put
my username and password in,

00:25:35.020 --> 00:25:37.520
submit the job, and it's just
essentially a rinse and repeat

00:25:37.520 --> 00:25:37.980
process.

00:25:37.980 --> 00:25:39.080
So that job is
submitted to Zync.

00:25:39.080 --> 00:25:40.255
The NUKE script is going up.

00:25:40.255 --> 00:25:42.224
Zync will make sure
all the assets needed

00:25:42.224 --> 00:25:43.390
for the render are up there.

00:25:43.390 --> 00:25:46.020
But of course, our CG was
already rendered on Zync,

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:48.060
so there's no
unnecessary file I/O.

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:50.012
And essentially,
that's the workflow.

00:25:50.012 --> 00:25:51.470
So again, I'm not
going to make you

00:25:51.470 --> 00:25:52.970
sit through the
entire render, but I

00:25:52.970 --> 00:25:55.809
can go ahead and very quickly
bring up my final output, which

00:25:55.809 --> 00:25:57.600
would just come back
to my output directory

00:25:57.600 --> 00:26:00.105
as soon as it's done, and
play that back for you.

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:09.090
And there you have it.

00:26:09.090 --> 00:26:13.050
So that's the Zync
workflow for you.

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:16.310
Try to make it as integrated as
possible, working within host

00:26:16.310 --> 00:26:17.480
applications.

00:26:17.480 --> 00:26:19.647
And again, it could
be very exciting today

00:26:19.647 --> 00:26:21.230
to hear the other
rendering solutions.

00:26:21.230 --> 00:26:23.563
I know Atomic Fiction has
done great work with Conductor

00:26:23.563 --> 00:26:26.230
IO, Thinkbox Deadline,
many of you use that,

00:26:26.230 --> 00:26:27.500
and the Avere folks as well.

00:26:27.500 --> 00:26:29.784
So we're really going for
the gamut of solutions here.

00:26:29.784 --> 00:26:31.450
And you're going to
be hearing about all

00:26:31.450 --> 00:26:32.824
of them coming up
this afternoon.

00:26:32.824 --> 00:26:34.700
So thank you.

